{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790307220,"nonce":"GhaPpf3wXSfwdVK1i1EBQHRZ848=","title":"Lua on Windows words OS failures with the system's text, not strerror's","message":"zylua worded a failed file operation from Rust's io::Error Display, split before \" (os error\". On Unix that text is strerror's, as the reference prints it. On Windows it is FormatMessage's (\"The system cannot find the file specified.\") and the number is a Windows system error code, while lua5.4 built for Windows goes through the C runtime and prints errno and strerror (\"No such file or directory\", 2). `load/files.lua` pins \"cannot open nothere.txt: No such file or directory\", so it fails on Windows; so would every io.open/os.remove/os.rename failure message.\n\nAlso: host_os's `fail` read GetLastError after C runtime calls rather than errno, and host_io built its EBADF and ESPIPE errors with from_raw_os_error, which Windows reads as system error codes 9 and 29.\n\nFix: map a Windows system error code to errno by the C runtime's own table (_dosmaperr) and word it with the C runtime's strerror; read errno after C runtime calls; carry an errno-valued error as its own type. Unix output is unchanged. Found by reading; the Windows job has not reached the Lua suite yet.","files":null}]}